# Godot .NET Assembly Reload — Pitfalls & Required Patterns

Hard-won rules for writing **editor plugin C#** in this project (Nebula addon and
any other `[Tool]` code). Violating them causes the `godot#78513` failure class:

```
ERROR: modules/mono/mono_gd/gd_mono.cpp:811 - .NET: Failed to unload assemblies.
ERROR: modules/mono/csharp_script.cpp:673 - Condition "managed_callable->delegate_handle.value == nullptr" is true.
ERROR: Error calling from signal 'pressed' to callable: 'ManagedCallableMiddleman::': Method not found.
```

Symptoms: failed/degraded assembly reloads, dead buttons after a reload, zombie
editor UI, NullReferenceExceptions in signal handlers. Root causes were found by
an 8-stage bisection (2026-07); background on the debugging technique:
https://dev.heavenlode.com/posts/debug-godot-assembly-unloading/

## How assembly reload actually behaves

When the editor hot-reloads C# (triggered by the build step of any editor play
or the Build button — **never** on window focus):

- Managed instances are **recreated in place**. `_EnterTree` / `_Ready` /
  `_ExitTree` do **NOT** re-run. Plain (non-`[Export]`) fields come back null.
- Signal connections backed by managed delegates (`+=`, `Callable.From`,
  lambdas) **die** — their GCHandles are freed; later invocations print the
  `managed_callable` / `ManagedCallableMiddleman` errors above. Those errors
  are residue of freed handles, not the cause of a failed unload.
- Name-bound callables (`new Callable(this, MethodName.X)`) **survive** and
  re-resolve against the recreated instance.
- Because `_ExitTree` never runs on reload, any cleanup that lives there does
  not happen. Cleanup that must run on reload belongs in `Dispose(bool)`.

## Rule 1 — Never park a delegate Callable on an object without a C# script

`Callable.From(...)` (and `+=` on signal events) creates a delegate-backed
callable holding a strong GCHandle into the current assembly. If that callable
is stored on a native singleton (`ProjectSettings`, `EditorSettings`, …) or a
GDScript node, nothing releases it on reload and it **pins the ALC** →
`Failed to unload assemblies`. Delegate callables held by C#-scripted nodes
are cleaned up by the reload machinery; ones on non-C# objects are not.

```csharp
// WRONG — pins the assembly on every reload:
ProjectSettings.Singleton.Connect("settings_changed", Callable.From(OnChanged));

// RIGHT — name-bound: no managed state, survives reloads:
ProjectSettings.Singleton.Connect("settings_changed", new Callable(this, MethodName.OnChanged));
```

Project policy: **all editor-UI signal connections are name-bound** — never
`+=` / `Callable.From` — so dead-middleman bugs are structurally impossible.
Private methods are fine; the source generators expose them.

## Rule 2 — The C# `Callable` struct cannot hold native custom callables

A bound/custom callable (e.g. from GDScript `bind()` or `Expression`) is
**silently nulled** by `Variant.AsCallable()` — the C# struct can only
represent object+method pairs and managed delegates. Consequences:

- `Callable.Bind` is not exposed in C#. To attach a bound argument (e.g. a row
  index) delegate-free, build it with a core `Expression` and keep it a
  `Variant` end to end, connecting via the Variant call path:

```csharp
private Variant BindIndex(StringName method, int index)
{
    var expression = new Expression();
    expression.Parse("c.bind(i)", new[] { "c", "i" });
    return expression.Execute(new Godot.Collections.Array { new Callable(this, method), index });
}
// Connect WITHOUT crossing the C# Callable struct:
button.Call("connect", "pressed", BindIndex(MethodName.OnRowPlayPressed, index));
```

- Callables read back from `GetSignalConnectionList` suffer the same loss:
  engine-internal (`callable_mp`) connections arrive null, and disconnect
  attempts fail. Track your own connections; don't sweep by heuristics.

## Rule 3 — Expect the zombie instance; self-heal

After any reload, an in-place-recreated instance has null fields while its old
UI nodes still sit in the tree. Patterns used in `Tools/Main.cs` and
`Tools/MainScreen/NebulaMainScreen.cs`:

- **Self-heal**: `Main._Process` treats `mainScreenInstance == null` as the
  post-reload signature → sweeps stale nodes by name → rebuilds the UI.
  (Processing enablement is baked at plugin init, so the first session after
  adding `_Process` needs an editor restart.)
- **Zombie guards**: handlers that can fire on a stale instance (surviving
  name-bound connections) null-check their UI fields and warn instead of
  throwing.
- **Reload-surviving state lives outside C#**: `nebula_play_orchestrator.gd`
  (@tool GDScript parented to the editor base control) owns play-session pids
  and the log buffer; the C# tab is a thin view that resyncs in `_Ready`.

## Debugging a failed unload

- Trigger reloads deterministically: touch a C# file, build externally, press
  the editor **Build** button. Reload never happens on focus.
- Bisect with const-bool kill-switch flags around plugin components; restart
  the editor between rounds (flag changes are inert without `_EnterTree`).
- For direct evidence: WinDbg/lldb + dotnet-sos, `dumpheap -type
  LoaderAllocator`, `gcroot` — see the blog post above.
